course: dictionary
lesson: 5204
title: Jan: a short form and a name
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Jan can mean two things, and context tells you which. When Jan is short for January, it is a short written form. It is used on calendars, forms and timetables when there is no space for the whole word. «Her foster mum, Jan, takes one look and fetches a proper suitcase with wheels.» Here there is a comma on each side, and Jan is a person, so it is a name.
explain: Look for clues. Is there a date, a number or a list of months nearby? Then Jan is probably the month. Is there a person doing something, like taking a look or fetching a case? Then Jan is probably a name. Always check by swapping in your meaning and seeing if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Jan is a short written form of January, the first month of the year.
fact: January has 31 days, and it is the first month on a calendar from Jan to Dec.
fact: Jan is also a first name. It is used for boys in Dutch, Polish and Czech.
fact: Jan can sometimes be short for the names Janet or Janice.
fact: The short form Jan is written with a capital J, because it stands for a name or a month name.
exercise: On a form, you write Jan in the date box. What does Jan stand for? | June, the sixth month of the year | Monday in a timetable | A girl's name | *January, the first month of the year
exercise: «Her foster mum, Jan, takes one look and fetches a proper suitcase with wheels.» In this sentence, what is Jan? | A month of the year | A short word for yesterday | A kind of suitcase | *A person's first name
exercise: Which is the short form of the first month of the year? | *Jan | Feb | Dec | Sept
test: Jan is the first month of the year. How many days does January have? | Thirty-two | Twenty-eight | Thirty | *Thirty-one
test: Why is Jan written with a capital J? | Because it means cold | Because it is a verb | Because it is always at the end of a sentence | *Because it is a short form of a month name, and names start with capital letters
exam: «In 1853 Ignacy Łukasiewicz, a Polish pharmacist, and Jan Zeh made a kerosene lamp in Lviv.» What is Jan in this sentence? | A month of the year | A hospital | *A first name for a man | A kind of kerosene
